<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Trachonitis</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Lajat</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Trachonitis was a rugged lava field south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, now known as the Lajat. Its caves sheltered brigands, and Augustus gave it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herod-the-great">Herod the Great</a> in the 20s BCE to suppress them. It later formed part of the tetrarchy of Philip and of the kingdoms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herod-agrippa-i">Agrippa I</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agrippa-ii">Agrippa II</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/trachonitis.jpg" alt="Trachonitis" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anas Qtiesh · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lajat,_Syria2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
